KNOWNBUG: [temp.spec]/4 + [temp.inst]/2 -- each specialization of a member
function template is a distinct entity.  When the template parameters are not
reflected in the parameter types (e.g. `template<class U> static unsigned sz()`),
CBMC names every specialization with the same unsuffixed member symbol (its
signature omits the template arguments), so sz<char> and sz<int> collide on one
symbol and share a single body; sz<int> therefore returns 1 (char's body) and
assertion 2 currently FAILS (unsound).  Reclassify CORE once such member
function template specializations get distinct symbols.  (A targeted fix that
suffixes member-function-template instance names with the template arguments was
prototyped but regressed std::function's type erasure, whose internal member
function templates are instantiated with lambda/local-type arguments whose
synthetic names get context-dependent suffixes; a robust fix needs a stable
per-specialization mangling.)
